== 1. nothing to measure must NOT render as a plausible zero ==
  PASS  a missing capture -> exit 2 and NO-MEASUREMENT
  PASS  an EMPTY capture -> exit 2 and NO-MEASUREMENT
== 2. a capture of nothing but startup probes is NO MEASUREMENT ==
  PASS  2 probes, 0 turns -> exit 2, and the probe COUNT is reported (counting lines would have called this a measurement)
== 3. the measurement discriminates: a known delta is reported as that delta ==
  PASS  +500 content bytes measured as exactly +500 (220 -> 720)
== 4. 100% field coverage, and it must BITE when a field is dropped ==
  PASS  fields + predicted framing == whole body, exactly
  PASS  the split NAMES every top-level field (5 of 5) -- a narrowed system/tools/messages decomposition fails here even if its own arithmetic closes
  PASS  framing is a function of the KEYS alone (60 B either way) -- it cannot absorb a dropped field, which is what makes the invariant falsifiable
  PASS  dropping `tools` from the split BREAKS the invariant (50 + 60 != 125)
== 5. an UNKNOWN top-level field fails LOUDLY and names itself ==
  PASS  unknown field -> exit 4 and the field is named
== 6. provider metadata is EXCLUDED from model input, and the split bites ==
  PASS  model input == body - provider metadata, and the metadata is non-zero (759 B) so the fixture can tell the two apart
  PASS  with no metadata present, model input == the whole body (no phantom deduction)
== 7. both protocols normalise into the same columns ==
  PASS  an Anthropic top-level `system` and an OpenAI role=system message both land in the system column, and neither is counted as conversation
  PASS  a `role: developer` message lands in the SAME column as `role: system` (49 B vs 46 B, the 3-byte difference being the role NAME) and the conversation column is identical -- the spelling OpenAI documents today
== 8. the model is read per request, not assumed for the capture ==
  PASS  three requests naming two models and no model -> three groups, including <ABSENT> rather than a default
== 9. faithfulness: reproducing passes, substituting another arm FAILS ==
  PASS  a capture checked against ITSELF reproduces every field
  PASS  checked against a DIFFERENT arm's capture it fails and names the field (so the check can fail, which is the only reason to trust it passing)
  PASS  a reference with no measurable request is CANNOT CHECK, not a pass
== 10. claim drift, and it must NOT fire on reflowed prose ==
  PASS  every claim found even though one WRAPPED across a line break -- the gate is whitespace-insensitive, which is why it does not cry wolf at reflow
  PASS  a changed digit IS caught -- so the whitespace tolerance did not cost the gate its teeth
  PASS  a missing prose file is CANNOT CHECK, not a pass
== 11. a capture line this reader cannot READ is refused, not measured around ==
  PASS  one unreadable line refuses the whole capture (a partial read would silently shrink every total)
  PASS  a record that is a list -> exit 2, naming the file and the LINE (it used to raise AttributeError and exit 1)
  PASS  a record that is a string -> exit 2, naming the file and the LINE (it used to raise AttributeError and exit 1)
  PASS  a `body` that is a list -> exit 2, naming the file and the LINE (it used to raise AttributeError and exit 1)
  PASS  a `body` that is a string -> exit 2, naming the file and the LINE (it used to raise AttributeError and exit 1)
  PASS  `body: null` is still a PROBE, not a structural fault -- the check refuses the wrong shape without refusing a legitimate record
  PASS  a REPEATED top-level key is named and refused (json.loads keeps the last value, so a 10 KB system prompt silently became 3 bytes with coverage green)
== 11b. the four columns the README publishes, against hand arithmetic ==
  PASS  hand-computed columns all match: system 12 + tools 15 = fixed 27 B, 1 tool, 1 message, and wire 12345 B read from the RECORD (not re-derived from the body, which is 113 B here -- so the two are now distinguishable)
  PASS  no overrun note when model input is below the wire figure
  PASS  when the normalised split EXCEEDS the wire figure, the report says so (unflagged, `fixed B` larger than `wire B` reads as a broken instrument)
== 11c. the claims pin EVERY arm, and the percentage names its denominator ==
  PASS  a two-arm capture yields claims for BOTH arms (10 claims over arm-one, arm-two) -- it used to pin only rows[0], leaving one whole engine unpinned
  PASS  the metadata share is computed over the BODY (96.5%), and this fixture's wire figure would give 3041.4% -- so the denominator is pinned, not assumed
== 11d. a request with tools but no messages is a TURN, not a probe ==
  PASS  a tools-only request is measured, not filed as a startup probe
== 11e. a refused capture is fatal even beside a good one ==
  PASS  a corrupt line beside a GOOD capture still exits 2 and prints no table -- a refused capture is a hole in the total whether or not another one was fine
== 11f. the fields whose LENGTH is published must be the right container ==
  PASS  a `tools` that is a dict is refused by name, not counted (len() of the wrong container counts the wrong thing and prints it as a schema count)
  PASS  a `messages` that is a string is refused by name, not counted (len() of the wrong container counts the wrong thing and prints it as a schema count)
== 11g. raw_bytes -- the only direct observation -- is validated ==
  PASS  raw_bytes absent -> exit 2, named (it used to reach a ZeroDivisionError or a `int += None` and exit 1)
  PASS  raw_bytes null -> exit 2, named (it used to reach a ZeroDivisionError or a `int += None` and exit 1)
  PASS  raw_bytes a string -> exit 2, named (it used to reach a ZeroDivisionError or a `int += None` and exit 1)
  PASS  raw_bytes negative -> exit 2, named (it used to reach a ZeroDivisionError or a `int += None` and exit 1)
  PASS  raw_bytes 0 beside a body that parsed -> exit 2, named (it used to reach a ZeroDivisionError or a `int += None` and exit 1)
== 11h. instruction roles are read in BOTH protocol branches ==
  PASS  a top-level `system` AND a role:system AND a role:developer message all land in the system column (18 + 183 = 201 B, +15 B of tools = 216 B fixed), leaving 1 conversation message of 35 B -- the Anthropic branch reads roles too, which it did not, and it reads ALL of them, which a six-character mutation showed nothing was checking
== 11i. an overrun BEYOND what separators explain is refused, not noted ==
  PASS  wire 101 B against a 113 B body (overrun 12, separators explain 11) -> refused by name
  PASS  wire 102 B against a 113 B body (overrun 11, separators explain 11) -> measured, with the note
== 11j. two captures under ONE arm label are refused, not merged ==
  PASS  two captures sharing one arm label -> exit 2, naming both files (they used to collapse into one row, with the second capture pinned by nothing)
  PASS  two requests in ONE capture are still measured (2 rows) -- the refusal is about two FILES claiming one label, not about a capture with history
== 12. an unavailable tokeniser is a reported state, never a crash ==
  PASS  a malformed --tokeniser spec is refused with a reason
  PASS  an unsupported tokeniser module is refused with a reason
  PASS  an encoder that is really len(bytes)//4 is REFUSED by the LOAD PATH rather than labelled tiktoken:cl100k_base -- the guard and its call site, not just the predicate
  PASS  all 6 fixed-divisor spellings are refused, not just the one literal expression (len//4, round(len/4), ceil(len/4), len//4 + 1, len//5, int(len/3.8)) -- the guard tests the CLASS, which is what docs/limits.md claims
  PASS  a count whose bytes-per-token ratio DOES vary across the probes passes
== 13. an UNPARSEABLE recorded body is refused, never filed as a probe ==
  PASS  3 unparseable records refuse the capture and their 144000 raw bytes are NAMED (they used to be reported as startup probes, with exit 0)
  PASS  is_probe() says an unparseable body is NOT a probe, which is the one-line distinction the whole refusal rests on
== 14. the coverage invariant's own COMPARISON, pinned ==
  PASS  coverage_holds is exact equality: a 10-byte DEFICIT fails and a 10-byte SURPLUS fails (`<=` would pass the deficit, which is the dropped-field case)
== 15. framing is right for keys that are not plain ASCII words ==
  PASS  multibyte and escape-needing keys: coverage still exact, and framing 84 B differs from the len(key)+2 shortcut's 69 B -- so the shortcut is a DETECTABLE revert

self-test: 55 pass, 0 fail
